diff --git a/PROGRESS.md b/PROGRESS.md
index 3f834f8..d4a3226 100644
--- a/PROGRESS.md
+++ b/PROGRESS.md
@@ -11,6 +11,11 @@ Datomic-style segments: up to N sorted datom keys under one key (keyed by the la
 - `cargo test -p triplox --lib segment::` : the three layout tests pass for sizes 1/2/4/64 and mixed layouts.
 - Equivalence test passes: 11 queries x (current basis, as-of basis) identical for sizes 1, 3, 16, 256, and the segmented layouts use strictly fewer SlateDB keys. Its edge fixture was rebuilt (it used to build ops with a placeholder ref value and zip them back together, which left some queries empty).
 
+- `cargo test -p triplox` passes both at the default size 1 and with `TRIPLOX_SEGMENT_SIZE=256` (643 tests). Getting there needed:
+  - `datoms_from_cdc_transaction` (src/slate/cdc.rs) decoded one datom per WAL entry key. A segment rewrite republishes datoms from older transactions, so CDC re-emitted them and every incremental-query test failed. It now decodes the whole segment and keeps only datoms whose tx eid is the newest in the batch, which is this transaction's. This is the one real semantic change segments force on the rest of the system.
+  - Ten indexer tests and one AE point-`get` asserted the physical one-key-per-datom layout; they now read through `KeyCursor`. No production code does a point `get` on a datom key (checked), only on the version key.
+  - The planner's key-count estimate scale is now derived from the env var once per process (`LazyLock`) instead of being written by every `Indexer`, and the three estimate tests scale their expectation. A per-database segment size therefore does not reach the planner - noted as a limitation.
+
 ## Next
 1. cargo test -p triplox (default size 1, and with TRIPLOX_SEGMENT_SIZE=256), clippy.
 3. A/B bench interleaved (size 1 vs best size, 3 each) plus one run at each other size; row-count check vs baseline.
